.planning/phases/06-dual-chart-rendering-time-sync/06-01-PLAN.md:57:Create the charts page and render the two stacked 1h candlestick charts. Task 1 builds `public/charts.html` (stacked `#btc-chart` / `#eth-chart` panes, pinned LWC v5.2.1 standalone CDN script, a minimal reachability link from the records page, and pane CSS with explicit heights). Task 2 adds `public/js/charts.js`, which fetches one bounded 30-day window of klines for both symbols through the existing `api()` chokepoint, maps `Kline` rows to LWC v5 candle data (unix-seconds `time`), and renders a BTC candlestick series in the top pane and an ETH candlestick series in the bottom pane. Time-sync is deliberately NOT wired here — plan 06-02 adds the logical-range sync + re-entrancy guard on top of the panes this plan renders.
.planning/phases/06-dual-chart-rendering-time-sync/06-01-PLAN.md:59:**Explicit non-goals (Phase 7 scope, not built here):** custom date range selection (CHART-05), record → chart deep link with 24h padding (CHART-06), log-scale toggle (CHART-04), and the shared nav bar (INFRA-06 → Phase 9). The page loads a fixed recent window on navigation only.
.planning/phases/06-dual-chart-rendering-time-sync/06-02-PLAN.md:15:  - "Browser access to the deployed `/charts.html` for the sync checkpoints. As in 06-01, the browser checkpoints require kline rows in D1 for the loaded window (Phase 2/3 data). If the dataset has no natural gaps, the edge-of-history (start of 2021) rapid-pan checkpoint still exercises the beyond-data-bounds logical-range path; the automated vitest suite additionally proves gap-handling by construction."
.planning/phases/06-dual-chart-rendering-time-sync/06-02-PLAN.md:22:    - "Initial alignment happens BEFORE linking: after both series setData(), the BTC chart's auto-fitted visible logical range is applied to the ETH chart once, then the two links are wired. This guarantees both charts start at the same bar-index span even when their datasets differ slightly (gaps), and avoids any feedback during the setup step."
.planning/phases/06-dual-chart-rendering-time-sync/06-02-PLAN.md:25:      provides: "createRangeSync() → { link(fromTimeScale, toTimeScale) } — a duck-typed bidirectional logical-range link with a shared re-entrancy guard and unusable-range rejection"
.planning/phases/06-dual-chart-rendering-time-sync/06-02-PLAN.md:31:      provides: "init() keeps the chart references, aligns the initial visible logical range, and wires sync.link(btcScale, ethScale) + sync.link(ethScale, btcScale)"
.planning/phases/06-dual-chart-rendering-time-sync/06-02-PLAN.md:44:# Plan 06-02 — Time Sync: logical-range link with re-entrancy guard, unit-tested and wired into both charts (CHART-03, CHART-08)
.planning/phases/06-dual-chart-rendering-time-sync/06-02-PLAN.md:48:Make the two panes from 06-01 move in lockstep. Task 1 adds `public/js/chart-sync.js` — a pure, duck-typed logical-range sync factory with a single shared re-entrancy guard (plus unusable-range rejection) — and a vitest suite whose fake time scale reproduces Lightweight Charts' synchronous notify-back to prove there is no feedback loop, no double-fire, and no crash under rapid events. Task 2 refactors `public/js/charts.js` to keep both chart references, align the initial visible logical range (BTC → ETH) before linking, and wire the two-directional link; then deploys and runs the interactive sync checkpoints, including a rapid-pan case and a cross-the-2021-data-boundary case.
.planning/phases/06-dual-chart-rendering-time-sync/06-02-PLAN.md:50:**Explicit non-goals (Phase 7 scope, not built here):** custom date range selection (CHART-05), deep link with 24h padding (CHART-06), log-scale toggle (CHART-04), and crosshair sync (explicitly out of scope per PROJECT.md "no chart interaction to feed the calculator" and PITFALLS.md #1608).
.planning/phases/06-dual-chart-rendering-time-sync/06-02-PLAN.md:56:- **CHART-03** maps to SC2. **CHART-08** (logical range, not time range) maps to both SC2 and SC3 and is enforced by the pure module using only logical-range APIs + the must-never-appear grep.
